  "id": "Compound::DB01236 - Gene::3776",
  "verbalization": "Sevoflurane, Desflurane, and Enflurane are all organofluorine anesthetics that share a common pathway involving the Kcnk2 channel. The evidence suggests that these compounds bind to Kcnk2, which is a potassium channel involved in the regulation of cellular excitability. The lowest common ancestors of Sevoflurane and its derivatives—Organofluorine Compound and Anesthetic Agent—highlight a shared pharmacological class, reinforcing the notion that these agents act through a common mechanism. The relationship between Sevoflurane and Kcnk2 is supported by the binding of these anesthetics to the channel, which is further evidenced by the causal link between Sevoflurane and Breath Holding Attack, mediated through Desflurane. The integration of these pathways underscores a consistent mechanism of action, where the anesthetic agents interact with Kcnk2 to modulate cellular function, potentially influencing respiratory and neurological processes.",
